### Version B - direct tests against the validation Azure Function
### These only exercise validate_expense's logic in isolation (scenarios
### 5 and 6, plus a sanity check on 1). For the full end-to-end pipeline
### (auto-approve, manager approval/rejection, timeout/escalation), use
### send_test_messages.py to put messages on the Service Bus queue and
### watch the Logic App run history in the Azure portal instead — Service
### Bus queues aren't reachable directly over HTTP.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:7071/api
@functionKey = REPLACE_WITH_LOCAL_FUNCTION_KEY_IF_NEEDED

### Scenario 1 sanity check: valid expense -> {"valid": true, "reason": null}
POST {{baseUrl}}/validate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "employee_name": "Alice Chen",
  "employee_email": "alice@example.com",
  "amount": 45.50,
  "category": "supplies",
  "description": "Notebooks and pens",
  "manager_email": "manager@example.com"
}

### Scenario 5: missing required fields -> {"valid": false, "reason": "Missing required field(s): employee_email, manager_email"}
POST {{baseUrl}}/validate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "employee_name": "Erin Wu",
  "amount": 60,
  "category": "meals",
  "description": "Team lunch"
}

### Scenario 6: invalid category -> {"valid": false, "reason": "Invalid category '...'..."}
POST {{baseUrl}}/validate
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "employee_name": "Frank Lopez",
  "employee_email": "frank@example.com",
  "amount": 120,
  "category": "entertainment",
  "description": "Client dinner",
  "manager_email": "manager@example.com"
}
